# Files whose BYTES are the record, not their text.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# ====================
# docs/SNAPSHOT.md (v1 study, now at tag v1-final) recorded a SHA-256 for the frozen predictor's .json. The file
# was committed from Windows, where git's core.autocrlf rewrote its CRLF line
# endings to LF on the way in. The result:
#
#     on the author's disk   694 bytes   FE280C38...   <- the recorded hash
#     in the repository      642 bytes   f4cfec1d...
#
# so a reviewer cloning on Linux computes a hash that does not match the table,
# and a reviewer on Windows with autocrlf gets it back. A fingerprint whose
# value depends on the reader's platform is not a fingerprint - and it would
# have failed in exactly the place it matters most, on the one artefact that
# makes the frozen-predictor claim checkable at all.
#
# The .pt escaped only because git auto-detected it as binary from its NUL
# bytes. That is luck, not a rule, so the rule is written down here.
#
# `-text` means: never convert, in either direction, on any platform. The
# committed bytes are the produced bytes.
w4_frozen_20hz_t150_train2345_s0.json -text
w4_frozen_20hz_t150_train2345_s0.pt   -text

# The same protection for every other artefact whose identity is its bytes.
# These are already detected as binary today; naming them means a future file
# that happens to start with printable characters cannot slip through.
*.pt   binary
*.mat  binary
*.slx  binary
*.slxc binary

# Generated text files. MATLAB and Python write them with LF; with core.autocrlf on Windows git then warned "LF will
# be replaced by CRLF" for each and listed them as modified after every regeneration. eol=lf keeps LF in the working
# copy on every platform, so a regeneration that changes nothing shows nothing.
paper/tables/*.md          text eol=lf
paper/figures/*.labels.txt text eol=lf
docs/RESULTS_P2.md         text eol=lf
data/SHA256SUMS.txt        text eol=lf
*.pdf binary
*.png binary
*.fig binary
